# Dice Resolution — Homebrew Configuration
#
# This file holds the configurable values used by the dice and resolution
# mechanics. Both the Ruby and C# implementations load this same file and
# translate the keys to their own naming conventions when reading them.
#
# Keys are written in human-readable form (capitalized, with spaces) to
# match the defined terms in the glossary.

# The number of sides on each die. All dice rolled in the system use this
# value.
Die Size: 10

# The default Target Number before any Target Number Modifiers are applied.
# This is the threshold a die must meet or exceed to count as a Success under
# neutral conditions.
Base Target Number: 6

# The lowest value a Target Number may take after all modifiers are applied.
# Bonuses that would push the Target Number below this value instead become
# Starting Successes (1 per point of overflow).
Minimum Target Number: 3

# The highest value a Target Number may take after all modifiers are applied.
# Penalties that would push the Target Number above this value instead become
# Starting Failures (1 per point of overflow).
Maximum Target Number: 9

# The lowest permitted Dice Count. Every Roll must use at least this many dice.
Minimum Dice Count: 6

# A value used to derive the maximum permitted Dice Count. The maximum equals
# the Minimum Dice Count plus the Dice Count Range, minus one. It also appears
# in Prowess-related calculations performed outside this module.
Dice Count Range: 5

# The maximum Competency Bonus that Compute Check Details will allocate before
# spilling further Prowess into Starting Value. Excess Prowess past the
# Maximum Dice Count fills the Competency Bonus up to this cap; anything left
# becomes Starting Value. The default 3 matches the headroom between the Base
# Target Number and the Minimum Target Number, so the cap allocates exactly as
# much Bonus as the TN floor can absorb before overflow conversion would kick
# in inside compute_roll_parameters.
Competency Bonus Cap: 3

# The number of Successes required for a typical Check to succeed. Specific
# Checks may require more, but this is the default.
Default Success Threshold: 2

# The number by which Failures must exceed Successes for a Fumble to occur on
# a typical Check. Specific Checks may require a greater excess, but this is
# the default.
Default Fumble Threshold: 2

# The list of accepted Target Number Modifier types, with a short description
# of each. The dice resolution module uses this list to validate modifier
# keys: a modifier key is valid if it has the form `<Type> Bonus`,
# `<Type> Penalty`, or `<Type> Starting` for some Type in this list.
#
# This does NOT police which types may appear as Bonuses vs Penalties vs
# Starting; anything in the list may have any of the three. Inherent, for
# example, appears here but is only ever a Bonus in practice — the
# restriction lives in the character/magic module, not in dice resolution.
#
# Adding a new type here lets it flow through the dice resolution system
# immediately without a code change. Removing one will cause callers who
# still pass that type to fail validation.
#
# When multiple modules need shared vocabulary, this list will move to a
# common config file.
Bonus Types List:
  Competency: "A modifier reflecting a creature's prowess and physical ability."
  Circumstance: "A modifier reflecting environmental conditions or other external factors. Typical source of direct Starting values."
  Morale: "A modifier reflecting the creature's emotional state, the effects of alchemical substances, or natural body chemistry."
  Guidance: "A modifier reflecting the most common magical effects."
  Inherent: "A modifier equal to a creature's Tier. Used for the natural bonus that comes with becoming more magical. Only ever appears as a Bonus."
  Ascendancy: "A modifier applied when a creature contends with a creature of a different Tier."
